Negative control for the ONE-testing-entry boundary (`eslint.config.mjs`,
`noWorkspaceSubpathImportsRule`), the lint half of the gate
`testing-entry-boundary.spec.ts` measures.

`packages/headless`'s `exports` map publishes exactly one test entry —
`"./testing"` — and no `"./testing/*"`. The rule's lookahead is what makes that
mechanical: it admits `testing$` and nothing below it, so a per-module specifier
reds from every position, the re-armed test lane (block 8h) included. Before
this control the lookahead also carried `|testing/`, which kept admitting a
surface the package had stopped publishing; the boundary was true by accident,
green because no caller happened to spell it any more.

This plants ONE offender: a per-module subpath import in a `__tests__/`
position, which is the MOST permissive lane in the repo for this specifier
(block 8h re-arms `./testing` there). If the ban holds in 8h it holds
everywhere, since 8g is strictly narrower.

  playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/__tests__/
    testing-entry-subpath-reintroduced.ts   (new file)
    `import ... from "@upmind-automation/headless/testing/client-email"`

The hunk ADDS a file rather than mutating a consumer on purpose: every real
consumer of the entry is mid-migration, and a context-free addition cannot rot
against that churn. Lint config is glob-driven, so a file at this path sits in
exactly the same lane a real spec does.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`
(never `git checkout --`).

@lint-run: the Then is the lint run's exit status, not a vitest assertion —
same realization as `packages/scenario-harness/src/__tests__/known-bad/`.

Reds: `./node_modules/.bin/eslint
playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/__tests__/testing-entry-subpath-reintroduced.ts`
reports `no-restricted-imports` on the planted line. A run that stays GREEN
means the lookahead admits `testing/` again and the one-entry ruling is back to
being true by accident. `git apply -R` must return the run to green.

